Doug Freyburger wrote:

> For a spell caster - Get create monster and altar
> camping will beat any protection racket.  Doesn't
> apply to many classes but it works better as it
> costs corpses not gold.

But beside buying protection, gold is pretty much
worthless and a devteam trap item for its weight.

You can empty a shop by several strategies that
leave the shopkeeper broke, destitute of goods, and
alive. Unless you're trying for a speed ascension,
the time taken is pretty much irrelevant. You just
need to make sure you have at least one pet. So,
gold for shops is unnecessary, and gold for the
oracle is a bit of an embellishment to a normal
game.

By the way, someone mentioned not _robbing_
Izchak???

I've been happily credit cloning him out of his eye
teeth (which is tedious unless you find a bunch of
lamps), I just make sure not to kill him, in my
current game by locking him in his shop.  Have the
rules gotten tighter?

I just went through Mines Town with a pet iron golem
(I found an early wand of polymorph), which pet was
really hard on the rest of the shopkeepers, and
after I'd brought the protection racket to closure
with the priestess, somehow she seemed to expire too
(I wanted the robe for my healer).

Normally I like to leave all shopkeepers and
priest(esse)s alive, but having a pet iron golem on
a cursed leash is very inconvenient to that plan.

How surprised I was to find out that I couldn't lock
that iron golem in a room in Mines Town to drop by
Izchak's place for a visit (he remains locked in his
shop so far this game). A cursed leash is capable of
pulling an iron golem through a locked door (which
is how I eventually noticed that the leash was
cursed; apparently you can't choke a pet that
doesn't breathe).

I managed to pray up some holy water on the
co-aligned altar and get the leash off before the
priestess died, but it was a near thing; she and the
iron golem were mixing it up pretty steadily until I
got him unleashed and could lock him out of the
temple so I could conduct business.

After I'd done the offerings and maxed my naked
armor class benefits, the priestess dropped some
17,000 zorkmids (a triple bones level at Mines 2 was
full of zorkmids, and I'd cleaned out at least two
shops outside of Mines Town too) when my iron golem
dropped back by the altar (as said above, I wanted
the priestess's robe).

Even with a blessed bag of holding (one of three on
that bones level), that's much money is far too much
cruft to be carrying around, so I left the zorkmids
in a chest on the altar.

Besides the iron golem, too slow to be a pet off of
a leash, I've currently got a large cat, two large
dogs, and two housecats, and so have been able to
hold my leveling up pretty much in check as I wander
back and forth between the main dungeon and Mines
Town.

Normal pets are good enough until about DLVL 8, when
the monsters are too buff for them to attack, I hope
to keep the iron golem going well beyond that. It's
at around 150 HP already, and I don't know where it
stops being able to level up; it's weilding a
dwarven mattock, a wonderful choice.

My PC is currently at HP: 36(36) Pw: 33(33) AC: -17
XP: 3/66 at T:8993.

The AC is built from protection plus a +0 robe, a +1
elven mithral coat, a +1 visored helmet, a +1 pair
of leather gloves, a +0 pair of jackboots, and, from
the bones level, apparently from a dead Valk, a +3
small shield, the only thing "special" in that list.

You could fairly say that my healer's out of the
spell casting business when foes are nearby, though
I could just about afford to ditch the mithral and
go without body armor for a while if that really
becomes an issue, the robe overcomes the small
shield's affects.

This is a nice XL to carry on to Sokoban, which I
haven't wanted to try with so many pets until
recently finally eating a floating eye and acquiring
a towel.

All in all, I'd say that's a pretty good
proof-by-example that extreme protection
racketeering (see below) can work just fine as part
of getting a PC off to a good start, and so is well
worth considering as part of anyone's overall game
plan.

I'd arrived at the Mines Town altar with 7 XP, after
a really tricky time getting past the triple bones
level without killing anything; I had to go through
three visits to the level to conquer it. My many
pets (lots tamed on the bones level) killed
everything else in the game but some newts I wanted
to kill for a possible mild buzz, and a jackel I
killed by running too fast, including some fairly
tough monsters like the three ghosts, three
shopkeepers, and a priestess, and the Mines
Town guards and watch captain (who had nothing
of value).

